Identical seeds replay byte-identical vector PDF ink; another seed changes
the physical samples. Reversing or reordering the accent's last stroke
changes its join endpoint, so the now unsafe connection refuses instead
of silently drawing a long pen bridge. The same explicit inverse glyph edit
recovers the original TSV byte-for-byte. Invalid spreads, missing pairs and
unsafe joins all leave the last accepted PDF and source TSV unchanged.

The test invokes the independent native Rust glyph and notebook CLI, not
mocked renderer functions, and never contacts a local printer. It does not
persist a joined cursive style, shape variants or sample parameters in a
saved semantic document. Browser-controlled page composition, analytic
curves, pen-lift identifiers and a complete personal script remain open.

